> **Note:** Memory differences come from temporary allocations in LINQ (enumerator, delegate, intermediate structures). The `Gen0/Gen1` GC pressure is significantly higher for LINQ.

## When Does It Matter?

| Scenario | Recommendation |
|---|---|
| **Hot path, called millions of times** | ✅ `for` loop or `Span<T>`/`Memory<T>` |
| **Called once at startup** | ✅ LINQ is fine; readability wins |
| **Filtering < 10K items** | ✅ LINQ; the difference is nanoseconds |
| **10M+ items, called frequently** | ✅ `for` loop, or better yet, **restructure** |
| **Need composability with other LINQ ops** | ✅ LINQ; premature optimization |
| **Parallelizable work** | ✅ `Parallel.For` or PLINQ |

## Even Better Alternatives

If performance truly matters at this scale, consider:

### 1. Pre-allocated Array with Index

```csharp
int[] filtered = new int[_data.Length]; // Worst-case size
int count = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < _data.Length; i++)
{
    if (_data[i] > 500)
    {
        filtered[count++] = _data[i];
    }
}
// Resize if needed, or keep as-is with a separate length variable
Array.Resize(ref filtered, count);
```
**Avoids all `List<T>` overhead.** Often **3–6x faster** than both approaches above.

### 2. `Span<T>` and `MemoryMarshal` (Most Modern)

```csharp
ReadOnlySpan<int> source = _data;
int[] result = new int[source.Length];
int count = 0;

foreach (int value in source)
{
    if (value > 500)
    {
        result[count++] = value;
    }
}
Array.Resize(ref result, count);
```

### 3. Parallel Processing

```csharp
// Using Parallel.For with local buffers to avoid contention
var results = new List<int>(estimatedSize);
var lockObj = new object();

Parallel.ForEach(
    Partitioner.Create(0, _data.Length),
    () => new List<int>(), // Local buffer per thread
    (range, state, local) =>
    {
        for (int i = range.Item1; i < range.Item2; i++)
        {
            if (_data[i] > 500)
                local.Add(_data[i]);
        }
        return local;
    },
    local => lock(lockObj) results.AddRange(local) // Merge
);
```

